<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Codex Amiatinus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Codex Amiatinus is the oldest surviving complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> Vulgate Bible. It was made at the twin monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monkwearmouth-jarrow-abbey">Wearmouth-Jarrow</a> in Northumbria in the early 8th century, one of three great Bibles commissioned by Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceolfrith">Ceolfrith</a>, who took it towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> as a gift for the pope in 716. It is now in the Laurentian Library in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> and takes its name from the abbey of Monte Amiata.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/codex-amiatinus.jpg" alt="Codex Amiatinus" width="320" height="445" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CodxAmiatinusFolio5rEzra.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
